#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064003 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "de1e3f3a-5a64-573f-8fb1-9952fa36a839" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Sine"}] :question/id #uuid "48ec6930-88b7-5aad-9bb7-c5e23e766f80" :question/math-academy-id "e-470" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{9}{4\\sqrt{1-9x^2}} \\, \\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "This integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-u^2}}\\textrm{d}u = \\arcsin(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u^2$ as the variable, we have $9x^2 = (3x)^2.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=3x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=3\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 1 3 \\,\\textrm d u=\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{9}{4\\sqrt{1-9{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{9}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(3x)^{2}}}\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{12}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot \\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡3x+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064004 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "227abe29-6e31-5afd-b0ca-3b8ed7d8c287" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Tangent"}] :question/id #uuid "3e89a278-54ab-59a6-adb6-38f43c0fbe9a" :question/math-academy-id "e-471" :question/problem "Calculate the integral  $\\displaystyle{\\int \\dfrac{3}{2 + 50x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x }.$" :question/worked-solution "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{3}{2 + 50x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x = \\int \\dfrac{3}{2(1 + 25x^2)} = \\dfrac 3 2 \\int \\dfrac{1}{1+25x^2}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+u^2}\\textrm{d}u =\\arctan u +C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u^2$ as the variable, we have $25x^2 = (5x)^2.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=5x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=5\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 1 5\\,\\textrm d u = \\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3}{2+50{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{3}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+25{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+(5x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}\\cdot \\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡5x+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064005 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2f9806d-3a72-5f0a-a6f4-db8dad8871f6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Definite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "cb771ff3-b5a9-5900-913f-df23957760b1" :question/math-academy-id "e-472" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle{\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt 2} \\dfrac{1}{2+x^2}\\, \\textrm{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{1}{2+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{1}{2(1+{x}^{2}/2)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{1}{2(1+(x/\\sqrt{2})^{2})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{1}{1+(x/\\sqrt{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that this integral now resembles the basic integral for the inverse tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int\\dfrac{1}{1+u^2}\\,\\textrm d u = \\arctan{u} + C.\n$$\n\nWe make the substitution $u=\\dfrac{x}{\\sqrt 2}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt 2}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\sqrt 2 \\,\\textrm d u = \\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\sqrt2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{1}{2+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{2}}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064014 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d18bdc7-9387-5b2d-98bd-015fa4a1eba7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Secant"}] :question/id #uuid "56860611-869d-5035-bf8f-f568c774d78d" :question/math-academy-id "e-5014" :question/problem "${\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{5\\, \\textrm{d}x}{|5x|\\sqrt{(5x)^2-1}}  = }$" :question/worked-solution "This resembles the basic integral for $\\textrm{arcsec},$\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^2-1}} \\textrm{d}u = \\textrm{arcsec}(u)+C.\n$$\n\nLet $u = 5x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=5\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n5\\,\\textrm{d}x = \\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5\\,\\text{d}x}{|5x|\\sqrt{(5x)^{2}-1}} & =\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}} \\\\  & =\\text{arcsec}(u)+C \\\\  & =\\text{arcsec}(5x)+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287470 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "de1e3f3a-5a64-573f-8fb1-9952fa36a839" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38b972ee-a867-51b2-a219-e9ccfd78ce49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287472} {:answer/id #uuid "99453bfd-a25b-5bc8-994d-7eed3cd45a7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287473} {:answer/id #uuid "3c7503b1-07cc-54df-9d22-28cf5115da96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287474} {:answer/id #uuid "5487628f-5c30-5a0d-be78-3425665e5133" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287475} {:answer/id #uuid "5e0ef784-2458-5a50-a83d-d0303278b839" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287476}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38b972ee-a867-51b2-a219-e9ccfd78ce49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287472} :answer-field/id #uuid "705c21dc-4764-585e-a0a3-ed10ace4c168" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287471}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1bbe4eea-ad7c-5a02-9125-5359b7a00ff4" :question/math-academy-id "q-114024" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{1-2{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "This integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $2{x}^{2}=(\\sqrt{2}x)^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\sqrt{2}x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\sqrt{2}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{1-2{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =4\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(\\sqrt{2}x)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{2}}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296126 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "de1e3f3a-5a64-573f-8fb1-9952fa36a839" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c321557-b8ab-599d-9637-eded56c518d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(5x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296128} {:answer/id #uuid "4b8a3275-2a3a-5283-92a3-5a3429d9d92b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{25}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x}{5})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296129} {:answer/id #uuid "cda93920-4941-5629-a5a6-dc7511496d5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x}{5})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296130} {:answer/id #uuid "03ebc035-5cdd-5d02-8738-0703aa5b130e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(5x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296131} {:answer/id #uuid "150f57bf-3e36-5795-b8dd-7f8d924537aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x}{5})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296132}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "150f57bf-3e36-5795-b8dd-7f8d924537aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x}{5})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296132} :answer-field/id #uuid "99bed4d2-ab4e-5786-80f9-abadf8d7a2fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296127}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a98b1cf8-b345-553f-a9f8-49e274be30db" :question/math-academy-id "q-114030" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{25\\cdot (1-{x}^{2}/25)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}/25}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x/5)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{5})}^{2}.$ \n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{5}.$ Differentiating, we get  \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{5}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=5\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{2}{5}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x/5)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot (5\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x}{5})+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296133 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "de1e3f3a-5a64-573f-8fb1-9952fa36a839" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06683b40-3991-5149-8a0a-86a938d43b3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\sqrt{x})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296135} {:answer/id #uuid "9a3bf19e-e0ab-5cb9-90b1-f43021992e92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296136} {:answer/id #uuid "6b0c7cee-5881-5103-bd91-3df14f29b289" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296137} {:answer/id #uuid "98a5a131-b799-5b3b-a0a3-c57bf567eeab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296138} {:answer/id #uuid "de7379ac-edf1-5569-8713-27d0beec1603" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296139}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "98a5a131-b799-5b3b-a0a3-c57bf567eeab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296138} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d1f923a-4ff1-53c6-af71-81359ee48ab3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296134}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7fa09994-b03e-519d-aa09-462602c3715d" :question/math-academy-id "q-114032" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{1-3{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "This integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $3{x}^{2}=(\\sqrt{3}x)^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\sqrt{3}x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\sqrt{3}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{1-3{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(\\sqrt{3}x)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x)+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296140 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "227abe29-6e31-5afd-b0ca-3b8ed7d8c287" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Tangent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "161035dd-534e-5c12-88df-5fe26ee6a1e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296142} {:answer/id #uuid "e8f91561-2994-50d4-8899-964b34007fb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296143} {:answer/id #uuid "6870e8fd-8f88-5aed-8b88-d157b85205d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296144} {:answer/id #uuid "79dd410d-7feb-59d2-8793-065ac1904021" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296145} {:answer/id #uuid "966fc9a3-c000-5205-81d7-a858502c46c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296146}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "79dd410d-7feb-59d2-8793-065ac1904021" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296145} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7b5e482-b9e5-5c06-9080-0da05e7a456c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296141}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "229d26fc-7842-5b85-86ac-4396d46bdbad" :question/math-academy-id "q-114045" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{8}{16+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{8}{16+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{8}{16\\cdot (1+{x}^{2}/16)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}/16}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+(x/4)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{4})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{4}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{4}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=4\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{8}{16+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+(x/4)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\cdot (4\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{1+{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287491 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "227abe29-6e31-5afd-b0ca-3b8ed7d8c287" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Tangent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c61400a3-b4de-54ea-b92a-2aaddcd20daf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(9x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287493} {:answer/id #uuid "28a8ac60-0116-5e76-8bd0-8240616a4ef3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{9})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287494} {:answer/id #uuid "742eb3c8-dc80-5fa9-ad36-9329b3c7002e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287495} {:answer/id #uuid "ed595cf4-8bc0-52f4-ad0d-ea6680beeb00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{9})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287496} {:answer/id #uuid "f60a0147-29d7-5482-8679-51b7608e7d2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{9})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287497}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f60a0147-29d7-5482-8679-51b7608e7d2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{9})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287497} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2c6f62f-0ddf-58c2-8ebd-7d6080a86152" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287492}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "773c49c1-4feb-58a3-abe3-1d48220dd1dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-114046" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{9}{81+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{9}{81+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{9}{81\\cdot (1+{x}^{2}/81)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}/81}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{1+(x/9)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\n The only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{9})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{9}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{9}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=9\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{9}{81+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{1+(x/9)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\cdot (9\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{1+{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{9})+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296147 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d18bdc7-9387-5b2d-98bd-015fa4a1eba7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Secant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41586ce1-c84e-52f5-afa7-4a8d0c32f0e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{arcsec}(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296149} {:answer/id #uuid "d8377633-bacf-5c7c-b510-899fe804aff3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{arcsec}(3x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296150} {:answer/id #uuid "74c30c11-17d0-5fed-aef3-6138bd144eb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\text{arcsec}(x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296151} {:answer/id #uuid "ea14f68c-bd33-5965-bba7-bb3e19cfd9e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{arcsec}(x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296152} {:answer/id #uuid "7dd4ad11-4f50-547b-a2a7-ac496d7c777a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{arcsec}(\\sqrt{3}x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296153}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7dd4ad11-4f50-547b-a2a7-ac496d7c777a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{arcsec}(\\sqrt{3}x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296153} :answer-field/id #uuid "8cc0e267-f80c-5ee2-a278-454fed19ebbd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296148}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bc2c1d59-0a7c-5002-a982-86e3a36461a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-114053" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{3{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{|x|\\sqrt{3{x}^{2}-1}}=\\int \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{|\\sqrt{3}x|\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{3}x)^{2}-1}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral now resembles the basic one for $\\text{arcsec},$\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\text{d}u=\\text{arcsec}(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $\\sqrt{3}x.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\sqrt{3}x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\sqrt{3}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{3{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{|\\sqrt{3}x|\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{3}x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}} \\\\  & =\\text{arcsec }u+C \\\\  & =\\text{arcsec}(\\sqrt{3}x)+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296154 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d18bdc7-9387-5b2d-98bd-015fa4a1eba7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Secant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5407476-01e9-587f-9a84-f414950ff22c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\sqrt{8}\\,\\text{arcsec}(x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296156} {:answer/id #uuid "f25c85c8-cf4d-5a21-9f06-5dd705afcaa7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{8}}{8}\\,\\text{arcsec}(x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296157} {:answer/id #uuid "cf01bf55-d339-5445-b4cb-46298db56091" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{arcsec}(\\sqrt{8}x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296158} {:answer/id #uuid "acde7078-c8ce-5bfb-b221-34f38c9d69bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\,\\text{arcsec}(\\sqrt{8}x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296159} {:answer/id #uuid "1088c4b9-da60-59cd-ab68-0910b963f941" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\,\\text{arcsec}(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{8}})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296160}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "acde7078-c8ce-5bfb-b221-34f38c9d69bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\,\\text{arcsec}(\\sqrt{8}x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296159} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d2a1f62-e46f-5be2-a82f-01cd0ef0da50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296155}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a33f4543-08d0-561d-b9e2-d065f565c833" :question/math-academy-id "q-114056" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{8}{|x|\\sqrt{8{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{8}{|x|\\sqrt{8{x}^{2}-1}}\\text{d}x=\\int \\frac{8\\sqrt{8}}{|\\sqrt{8}x|\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{8}x)^{2}-1}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral now resembles the basic one for $\\text{arcsec},$\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\text{d}u=\\text{arcsec}(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $\\sqrt{8}x.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\sqrt{8}x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\sqrt{8}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{8}}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{8}{|x|\\sqrt{8{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{8\\sqrt{8}}{|\\sqrt{8}x|\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{8}x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{8}\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{8}}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{8}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{8}}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}} \\\\  & =8\\,\\text{arcsec }u+C \\\\  & =8\\,\\text{arcsec}(\\sqrt{8}x)+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296161 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2f9806d-3a72-5f0a-a6f4-db8dad8871f6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Definite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44f31708-7364-5b00-bc40-83773c796def" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296163} {:answer/id #uuid "c8be7c0f-6c6c-5515-aa19-d61eb40f1685" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296164} {:answer/id #uuid "9320b7ee-1d53-5394-885b-36f361f9eed1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296165} {:answer/id #uuid "0b740056-8d11-5c1e-837f-82dfab08f2f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi }{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296166} {:answer/id #uuid "a2122e1b-e597-5789-a69c-dbb150b6008f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296167}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b740056-8d11-5c1e-837f-82dfab08f2f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi }{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296166} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd9358d7-9edc-56fd-b26d-a923c3cd4f30" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296162}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5a2898ff-e121-5eb2-975f-1ad5f1f98f96" :question/math-academy-id "q-114084" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2}{3+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2}{3+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2}{3\\cdot (1+{x}^{2}/3)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}/3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+(x/\\sqrt{3})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2}{3+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+(x/\\sqrt{3})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}/3}\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\cdot (\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}/3}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{1+{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u{|}_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}/3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi }{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296168 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2f9806d-3a72-5f0a-a6f4-db8dad8871f6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Definite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8fe2ab70-01b2-54e1-8289-39680848e275" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296170} {:answer/id #uuid "ec351c8a-a59a-52e6-ab72-a58ed58cdf16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296171} {:answer/id #uuid "ef52134e-bcee-553e-aa98-461e45e2d4cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296172} {:answer/id #uuid "05f0aba8-7b40-53ac-b543-7d8a81e824ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296173} {:answer/id #uuid "9906929e-8059-5eaf-9d4b-e49539f230a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296174}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9906929e-8059-5eaf-9d4b-e49539f230a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296174} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e56ad18-9be3-5335-8236-c82a7b2fc612" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296169}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "af2a59c4-813e-5800-8709-a92b89fd1ce1" :question/math-academy-id "q-19395" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{-0.25}^{0.25}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3-12{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-0.25}^{0.25}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3-12{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =3{\\int }_{-0.25}^{0.25}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3\\cdot (1-4{x}^{2})}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}}{\\int }_{-0.25}^{0.25}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-4{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{-0.25}^{0.25}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(2x)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(2x)}^{2}.$   \n\nSo, let's substitute $u=2x.$ Differentiating, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $-0.25$ | $0.25$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-0.5$ | $0.5$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-0.25}^{0.25}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3-12{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{-0.25}^{0.25}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(2x)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{-0.5}^{0.5}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}{\\int }_{-0.5}^{0.5}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u{|}_{-0.5}^{0.5} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(0.5)-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-0.5)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287526 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "227abe29-6e31-5afd-b0ca-3b8ed7d8c287" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Tangent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "917cb601-d5eb-572d-bf4f-bb06a638fc75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287528} {:answer/id #uuid "b1e2fd85-f260-5896-b563-68fad839d972" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4x)}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287529} {:answer/id #uuid "6d26f927-4703-5484-9b89-b190a8cfc990" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4x)}{16}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287530} {:answer/id #uuid "0d211053-2cb4-527f-9855-1c711ef74364" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(8x)}{8}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287531} {:answer/id #uuid "09012162-0dcf-5502-acf8-652fbf5f4664" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(16x)}{16}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287532}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b1e2fd85-f260-5896-b563-68fad839d972" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4x)}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287529} :answer-field/id #uuid "2979d981-dd42-57b5-88bf-67d3e7add18c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287527}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2793cfa5-af9d-509f-b5fa-c5da2d7d9995" :question/math-academy-id "q-2504" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{1}{1+16{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "This integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $16{x}^{2}=(4x)^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=4x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{1+16{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{1+(4x)^{2}}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4x)}{4}+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287540 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2f9806d-3a72-5f0a-a6f4-db8dad8871f6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Definite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00a4a8f7-42d1-5dd3-b7bb-4185824a00e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287542} {:answer/id #uuid "71112578-89b5-5fba-bc90-fd5415f8439e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}\\sqrt{5}\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287543} {:answer/id #uuid "d4734356-652b-5a14-8c19-972dd967f7ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}\\pi}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296175}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71112578-89b5-5fba-bc90-fd5415f8439e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}\\sqrt{5}\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287543} :answer-field/id #uuid "fee69010-2c06-5927-a264-74e8018dfe87" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287541}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6840375a-3412-588f-aa1f-b3b36a2c5359" :question/math-academy-id "q-351291" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{1/8}\\frac{10}{\\sqrt{5-80{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1/8}\\frac{10}{\\sqrt{5-80{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =10{\\int }_{0}^{1/8}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5(1-16{x}^{2})}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{\\sqrt{5}}{\\int }_{0}^{1/8}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-16{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1/8}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(4x)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine, \n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(4x)}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=4x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1/8}\\frac{10}{\\sqrt{5-80{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1/8}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(4x)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u\\,{|}_{0}^{1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{5}}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296176 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2f9806d-3a72-5f0a-a6f4-db8dad8871f6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Definite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "78be4c84-ed41-5da4-bd68-0bd038021517" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296178}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78be4c84-ed41-5da4-bd68-0bd038021517" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296178} :answer-field/id #uuid "631d3942-73e5-593d-b0d5-41181fe459b0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296177}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "be8bfccc-b567-5c62-9ea9-eace311d32ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-351827" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{3\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{15}{\\sqrt{36-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{15}{\\sqrt{36-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =15{\\int }_{0}^{3\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{36\\cdot (1-{x}^{2}/36)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{3\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}/36}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x/6)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{6})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{6}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{6}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=6\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $3\\sqrt{3}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{15}{\\sqrt{36-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{5}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x/6)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot 6\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =15{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}/2}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}} \\\\  & =15\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u\\,{|}_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}/2} \\\\  & =15\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})-15\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(0) \\\\  & =15\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =5\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296179 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2f9806d-3a72-5f0a-a6f4-db8dad8871f6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Definite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e7e9529f-31a1-5889-89d9-2a0638e13205" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296181}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7e9529f-31a1-5889-89d9-2a0638e13205" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296181} :answer-field/id #uuid "28c9e76d-6504-5a1a-a283-636dfd935599" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296180}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b13cfa7c-a8cd-51fa-8479-e970b2061f28" :question/math-academy-id "q-351835" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{1/12}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{1-36{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1/12}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{1-36{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{0}^{1/12}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(6x)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $(6x)^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=6x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=6\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{6}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{12}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1/12}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{1-36{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1/12}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(6x)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\cdot (\\frac{1}{6}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u{|}_{0}^{1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{18}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296182 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "227abe29-6e31-5afd-b0ca-3b8ed7d8c287" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Tangent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f60cb49-c9f6-581c-977a-79324ee3b3cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296184} {:answer/id #uuid "a4dc3cea-198a-5efa-835e-10ba0dbcf4b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296185} {:answer/id #uuid "3ae0ebc8-2f4e-5603-aa1f-1b2d58ed1c5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296186} {:answer/id #uuid "b674bb5a-c3e8-52f6-9db4-70c1b0c2bade" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296187} {:answer/id #uuid "a7655bba-315f-5d0e-8678-cb96c5e49d16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296188}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4dc3cea-198a-5efa-835e-10ba0dbcf4b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296185} :answer-field/id #uuid "a58b9f12-f23a-5c30-94d8-692f32f45430" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296183}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "32195101-2029-523b-86e7-1b542af1b41c" :question/math-academy-id "q-49505" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{1}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{4\\cdot (1+{x}^{2}/4)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}/4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1+(x/2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}.$ \n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{2}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=2\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1+(x/2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\cdot (2\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{1+{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296189 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "de1e3f3a-5a64-573f-8fb1-9952fa36a839" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec106e93-cc17-596b-bb1d-905975e1eaaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(4x)}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296191} {:answer/id #uuid "6a13fdde-6217-5e84-992e-c20461d684fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x)}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296192} {:answer/id #uuid "eac18b02-26e7-5c93-ab5e-a640af123a5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(4x)}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296193} {:answer/id #uuid "51fe00fb-90e9-5c18-b380-6ba59da0291a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4x)}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296194} {:answer/id #uuid "513fd128-3eaf-5542-affc-8da1c9604fc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(4x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296195}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec106e93-cc17-596b-bb1d-905975e1eaaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(4x)}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296191} :answer-field/id #uuid "435e26b2-b932-5641-af4d-d48eb725fa47" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296190}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d3cc9f37-e91e-5ae4-90cc-68725fe7b8a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-49535" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{1-16{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "This integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $16{x}^{2}=(4x)^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=4x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-16{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(4x)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(4x)}{2}+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287551 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "227abe29-6e31-5afd-b0ca-3b8ed7d8c287" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Tangent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83968c12-e703-5226-be8b-0715c08ed100" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x}{5})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287553} {:answer/id #uuid "7db732e0-f117-5d15-8e6c-192e1b8b0c88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(5x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287554} {:answer/id #uuid "b8363bbb-cc56-5ac6-8d3f-8e98593234ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{5})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287555} {:answer/id #uuid "eb4c1463-6ab8-54a2-86a8-95b8ee5aa49e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{5})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287556} {:answer/id #uuid "53788ea9-2d45-508b-8be1-dbc999162a1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(5x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287557}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8363bbb-cc56-5ac6-8d3f-8e98593234ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{5})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287555} :answer-field/id #uuid "968c70b5-c733-5603-a441-c96bb891841e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287552}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ce76c021-250c-5a67-b73f-f7d925e67c4d" :question/math-academy-id "q-49550" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{1}{25+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{25+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{25\\cdot (1+{x}^{2}/25)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}/25}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}\\int \\frac{1}{1+(x/5)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{5})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{5}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{5}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=5\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{25+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{25}\\int \\frac{1}{1+(x/5)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\cdot (5\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{1+{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{5})+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296196 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d18bdc7-9387-5b2d-98bd-015fa4a1eba7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Secant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46957e03-babc-5006-941b-1dff564d17d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(9x)}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296198} {:answer/id #uuid "129371b6-76e9-5934-946e-e72a122be68a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{arcsec}(x)}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296199} {:answer/id #uuid "e3d58318-7ff7-57d8-95e8-e8ec001031d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{arcsec}(9x)}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296200} {:answer/id #uuid "7ae53925-8b35-560d-ab46-9848d465e7c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(3x)}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296201} {:answer/id #uuid "b9090071-07b0-580b-b509-0f269fd31e38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{arcsec}(3x)}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296202}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b9090071-07b0-580b-b509-0f269fd31e38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{arcsec}(3x)}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296202} :answer-field/id #uuid "62ecf06b-a040-58ea-a505-42586cff8955" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296197}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e23bb3f4-a65e-57d7-abc7-33852f454847" :question/math-academy-id "q-49554" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{1}{3|x|\\sqrt{9{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{3|x|\\sqrt{9{x}^{2}-1}}=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{|3x|\\sqrt{(3x)^{2}-1}}.\n$$\n\nThis integral now resembles the basic one for $\\text{arcsec},$\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\text{d}u=\\text{arcsec}(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $3x.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=3x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{3|x|\\sqrt{9{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{|3x|\\sqrt{(3x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\text{arcsec }u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{arcsec}(3x)}{3}+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296203 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d18bdc7-9387-5b2d-98bd-015fa4a1eba7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Secant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c21e461-a671-5e4b-a962-f5a71b3900c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{arcsec}(7x)}{7}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296205} {:answer/id #uuid "738cd36e-d0da-5231-93f2-6de34c018b34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{arcsec}(7x)}{49}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296206} {:answer/id #uuid "25a79dbd-654f-5b3a-915d-8352e289b51a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{arcsec}(49x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296207} {:answer/id #uuid "3558b5a2-0c0d-54d9-83b8-f17385e8fc2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{arcsec}(49x)}{7}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296208} {:answer/id #uuid "e52250e6-c7fd-516d-9115-6e551b14a690" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{arcsec}(7x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296209}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e52250e6-c7fd-516d-9115-6e551b14a690" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{arcsec}(7x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296209} :answer-field/id #uuid "daf9c59a-5ecf-573b-9086-84dd83b422b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296204}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "975dd1af-9c7a-51cf-ad92-4355074f5c35" :question/math-academy-id "q-69475" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{7\\,\\text{d}x}{|7x|\\sqrt{(7x)^{2}-1}}=$" :question/worked-solution "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{7\\,\\text{d}x}{|7x|\\sqrt{(7x)^{2}-1}}=7\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{|7x|\\sqrt{(7x)^{2}-1}}.\n$$\n\nThis integral now resembles the basic one for $\\text{arcsec},$\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\text{d}u=\\text{arcsec}(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $7x.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=7x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=7\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{7}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}7\\int \\frac{1}{|7x|\\sqrt{(7x)^{2}-1}}\\text{d}x & =7\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{7}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}} \\\\  & =\\text{arcsec }u+C \\\\  & =\\text{arcsec}(7x)+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064851 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d18bdc7-9387-5b2d-98bd-015fa4a1eba7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Secant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89246796-f084-535b-9bcf-74cb453b66b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{arcsec}(64x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291737} {:answer/id #uuid "71ac8127-e14e-56c4-a515-c5897606da56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\text{arcsec}(8x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291738} {:answer/id #uuid "2a5e0987-5dd6-58f6-afd4-e850f1bd4974" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{arcsec}(8x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291739} {:answer/id #uuid "a256eb39-0a5c-5bdf-b751-4a56f59a8ef3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{arcsec}(4x)}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291740} {:answer/id #uuid "a42e9ab2-f8e1-5790-8b1e-01e07d816281" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{arcsec}(8x)}{8}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291741}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a5e0987-5dd6-58f6-afd4-e850f1bd4974" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{arcsec}(8x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291739} :answer-field/id #uuid "b69123c2-7d5f-5bb8-933f-37f2977e820c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291736}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1c758c6a-6697-5dfb-b64a-bc653c6c226b" :question/math-academy-id "q-69476" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{8\\,\\text{d}x}{|8x|\\sqrt{(8x)^{2}-1}}=$" :question/worked-solution "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{8\\,\\text{d}x}{|8x|\\sqrt{(8x)^{2}-1}}=8\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{|8x|\\sqrt{(8x)^{2}-1}}.\n$$\n\nThis integral now resembles the basic one for $\\text{arcsec},$\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\text{d}u=\\text{arcsec}(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $8x.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=8x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=8\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{8}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}8\\int \\frac{1}{|8x|\\sqrt{(8x)^{2}-1}}\\text{d}x & =8\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{8}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}} \\\\  & =\\text{arcsec }u+C \\\\  & =\\text{arcsec}(8x)+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296210 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "de1e3f3a-5a64-573f-8fb1-9952fa36a839" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae7ad0b5-0f5d-51ec-80e0-4c5e74c097c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296212} {:answer/id #uuid "c3587aac-e2c7-570c-b0c8-5d9c40e9811e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296213} {:answer/id #uuid "ac620c10-f521-5ff3-8b4f-b70b607db43c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296214} {:answer/id #uuid "250373ad-04fb-5674-bdcc-07079672e586" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296215} {:answer/id #uuid "56a6922a-5caa-5eb4-a801-d14114a20f17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296216}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "250373ad-04fb-5674-bdcc-07079672e586" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296215} :answer-field/id #uuid "674e39a4-d87d-57f7-812f-16c60879dddb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296211}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8c1e95af-d9b3-56dc-a410-8e208dc56f66" :question/math-academy-id "q-73712" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{8}{3\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:   \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{8}{3\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{8}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4\\cdot (1-{x}^{2}/4)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}/4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x/2)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}.$ \n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{2}.$ Differentiating, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=2\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\n \n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{8}{3\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{4}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x/2)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot (2\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
